WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:03.930
The next thing that I want to talk about in texture

2
00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:06.209
optimizations is the concept of mipmapping,

3
00:00:06.209 --> 00:00:09.210
is understanding what mipmaps are and why they're needed

4
00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:10.730
is of vital importance.

5
00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:14.169
Because mipmaps can have a direct impact

6
00:00:14.169 --> 00:00:15.730
on how your project performs.

7
00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:18.649
And understanding how you can manipulate mipmaps

8
00:00:18.649 --> 00:00:23.850
can allow you to perform some really amazing performance

9
00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:26.170
adjustments without having to sacrifice

10
00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:29.609
the quality of the final rendered pixels.

11
00:00:29.609 --> 00:00:32.409
Now, mipmaps are automatically generated

12
00:00:32.409 --> 00:00:37.049
when you import any texture into Unreal Engine 5,

13
00:00:37.049 --> 00:00:40.609
granted that that texture meets the power of two requirements

14
00:00:40.609 --> 00:00:42.649
in the x and the y-axis.

15
00:00:42.649 --> 00:00:44.810
When you import a texture behind the scenes,

16
00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:47.929
Unreal is going to generate what's known as a mipchain.

17
00:00:47.929 --> 00:00:51.049
Now, a mipchain contains multiple levels

18
00:00:51.049 --> 00:00:53.450
of the same image, with each level

19
00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:56.530
being half the resolution of the level before it.

20
00:00:56.530 --> 00:00:58.329
In the image on our screen in front of us,

21
00:00:58.369 --> 00:01:01.689
we can see a texture that is at a size of 4,096.

22
00:01:01.689 --> 00:01:05.409
And then the next mip down would be 2,048, and then

23
00:01:05.409 --> 00:01:12.370
1,024, 512, 256, 128, all the way down to 4 by 4.

24
00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:15.329
The reason this is done is so that when a texture is

25
00:01:15.329 --> 00:01:16.689
viewed very close to the camera,

26
00:01:16.689 --> 00:01:18.930
we can view the highest resolution version of it.

27
00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:22.049
And then as that camera and then as that texture

28
00:01:22.049 --> 00:01:24.329
gets further and further away from the camera,

29
00:01:24.329 --> 00:01:27.450
we can utilize a lower and lower resolution

30
00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:30.010
version of it, which helps us not only

31
00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:33.209
make that texture blend with the detail and the background

32
00:01:33.209 --> 00:01:37.129
better, but also use less rendering performance.

33
00:01:37.129 --> 00:01:40.370
Now, this is an example of mipmapping in action.

34
00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:42.129
On the left-hand side, we have a texture

35
00:01:42.129 --> 00:01:43.129
with mipmapping on.

36
00:01:43.129 --> 00:01:45.170
And the right-hand side, same texture, this time

37
00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:46.810
with mipmapping turned off.

38
00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:48.769
And the main difference we can see here

39
00:01:48.769 --> 00:01:53.489
is that as this texture starts to go from the foreground

40
00:01:53.489 --> 00:01:55.290
to the mid-ground to the background,

41
00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:56.930
notice that on the left-hand side,

42
00:01:56.969 --> 00:01:59.370
the texture seems to converge fairly nicely.

43
00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:01.530
We can kind of make out some of the detail,

44
00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:03.969
as we would expect, especially if we were looking

45
00:02:03.969 --> 00:02:05.250
at something in the real world.

46
00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:06.769
However, on the right-hand side,

47
00:02:06.769 --> 00:02:09.409
as the texture starts to go off into the distance,

48
00:02:09.409 --> 00:02:13.169
while things do converge, there is a lot of noise in there.

49
00:02:13.169 --> 00:02:15.569
And it makes it very hard to focus on anything.

50
00:02:15.569 --> 00:02:18.569
And this is why mipmapping is so important.

51
00:02:18.569 --> 00:02:21.610
Not only does mipmapping help your textures, especially

52
00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:25.289
textures in the distance, look like they blend together,

53
00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:27.650
it also makes things cheaper to render

54
00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:29.930
for those textures that are viewed in the distance.

55
00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:31.729
Now, you might be thinking, well, what do you mean by

56
00:02:31.729 --> 00:02:33.409
makes it cheaper to render?

57
00:02:33.409 --> 00:02:35.169
Well, this is what I'm talking about.

58
00:02:35.169 --> 00:02:37.370
Here we have the ancient temple demo,

59
00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:41.129
and we've taken that texture from our first slide

60
00:02:41.129 --> 00:02:44.849
and applied it to all of the static meshes in the world.

61
00:02:44.849 --> 00:02:48.330
As you can see, the 4096 version of our mipmap

62
00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:50.849
or the highest resolution of that mipmap

63
00:02:50.849 --> 00:02:53.129
isn't actually utilized all the time.

64
00:02:53.129 --> 00:03:00.340
In fact, we're more looking at 2048, 512, 128, and 256.

65
00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:01.939
This is really important to understand,

66
00:03:01.939 --> 00:03:05.500
is oftentimes when you want to go utilize an 8K texture

67
00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:07.580
or even a 4K texture, the only way

68
00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:10.180
to really see that whole version mipmap

69
00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:13.300
is to view that texture very, very close.

70
00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:16.219
Let's take a look at this inside of the editor in action.

71
00:03:16.219 --> 00:03:18.780
So here I have a spectral texture set up

72
00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:20.699
that's 1024 by 1024.

73
00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:23.860
And let's take a look at the texture in the background

74
00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:27.259
here as I start to get close to the one in the foreground.

75
00:03:27.259 --> 00:03:30.219
Notice that the cube is going to be changing as I

76
00:03:30.219 --> 00:03:31.580
get closer and closer to it.

77
00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:34.099
We're going to 512, 1024 by 1024.

78
00:03:34.099 --> 00:03:37.419
But notice as soon as I start to back up just even

79
00:03:37.419 --> 00:03:40.340
a little bit, we're going to start getting that 512,

80
00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:42.259
and then we're going to start getting

81
00:03:42.259 --> 00:03:45.539
the 256 and then the 128.

82
00:03:45.539 --> 00:03:49.740
So again, mipmapping is responsible for what

83
00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:51.180
you're seeing or that transition

84
00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:54.060
between the various levels of that texture.

85
00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:56.740
And if your texture meets the power of two requirements

86
00:03:56.740 --> 00:03:58.939
and the x and the y-axis, you'll

87
00:03:58.939 --> 00:04:01.419
have no problems actually using it

88
00:04:01.419 --> 00:04:03.020
because Unreal will do this for you

89
00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:06.580
all behind the scenes on import.
